WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:05.891
- Hello, thank you for joining us

00:00:05.891 --> 00:00:09.601
for this special live
session of Science News now,

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:10.901
on what it's like for journalists

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:12.901
to cover the biggest story of our lives,

00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:15.281
the Coronavirus Pandemic.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:16.671
We'll be talking about the challenges

00:00:16.671 --> 00:00:20.151
of covering this huge story
while also living through it

00:00:20.151 --> 00:00:22.761
and we'll be answering audience questions.

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:26.331
So please ask them in
the Q&amp;A chat section.

00:00:26.331 --> 00:00:27.761
I'm thrilled to introduce you

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.951
to the six amazing
Science News journalists

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:32.721
who have led our coverage of the pandemic.

00:00:32.721 --> 00:00:34.711
I'll let them introduce themselves.

00:00:34.711 --> 00:00:37.761
Aimee, let's start with you.

00:00:37.761 --> 00:00:40.071
- Hi, I'm Aimee Cunningham,

00:00:40.071 --> 00:00:42.391
I cover health and biomedicine.

00:00:42.391 --> 00:00:47.101
I've written about kids and
COVID and COVID vaccines

00:00:47.101 --> 00:00:50.431
among other stories, and the past year,

00:00:50.431 --> 00:00:53.641
I just finished a large project
that takes a historical look

00:00:53.641 --> 00:00:57.174
at infectious diseases and
epidemics over the past century.

00:01:01.031 --> 00:01:04.421
- I'm Erin Garcia de Jesus,
I am a staff writer here

00:01:04.421 --> 00:01:08.871
and I have mainly been
covering viral variants,

00:01:08.871 --> 00:01:11.221
I have a background in
evolutionary virology,

00:01:11.221 --> 00:01:13.521
so I've been writing a lot
about the virus' evolution

00:01:13.521 --> 00:01:18.504
as well as vaccines and how
virus variants and vaccines

00:01:18.504 --> 00:01:19.377
are interacting.

00:01:23.321 --> 00:01:24.551
- I'm John Lambert,

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:27.241
I'm also a staff writer
here at Science News

00:01:27.241 --> 00:01:30.761
and I focus more on the public
health side of the pandemic.

00:01:30.761 --> 00:01:33.841
So early on I was covering
a lot about testing

00:01:33.841 --> 00:01:37.801
and contact tracing, and
these days I've been focused

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:42.148
a lot on vaccine equity,
both in the US and abroad.

00:01:44.831 --> 00:01:46.441
- Hi, I'm Laura Sanders,

00:01:46.441 --> 00:01:49.551
the neuroscience reporter at Science News.

00:01:49.551 --> 00:01:52.671
I've been focusing on the
brain and how the virus

00:01:52.671 --> 00:01:55.231
might or might not
affect the brain directly

00:01:55.231 --> 00:01:58.561
and how stress from the
pandemic has interfered

00:01:58.561 --> 00:02:00.521
with all sorts of things.

00:02:00.521 --> 00:02:03.421
I've also covered how
shutdowns and school closures

00:02:03.421 --> 00:02:07.624
affected kids, both mental
health and academic performance.

00:02:12.181 --> 00:02:13.581
- Hi, I'm Sujata Gupta

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:15.781
I'm the social sciences writer

00:02:15.781 --> 00:02:18.661
and I've covered a range of things

00:02:18.661 --> 00:02:23.291
involving people's psychology
and mental health, behavior,

00:02:23.291 --> 00:02:27.921
risk perception, along with disparities,

00:02:27.921 --> 00:02:31.004
social and educational level disparities.

00:02:33.131 --> 00:02:35.071
- Hi, I'm Tina Hesman Saey

00:02:35.071 --> 00:02:39.111
I'm one of the senior
writers here at Science News,

00:02:39.111 --> 00:02:41.641
my bead is molecular biology,

00:02:41.641 --> 00:02:43.831
but the past two years I've been covering

00:02:43.831 --> 00:02:46.911
mainly many aspects of COVID,

00:02:46.911 --> 00:02:51.498
including drug treatments, vaccines,

00:02:52.681 --> 00:02:54.661
how the virus affects the body

00:02:55.761 --> 00:02:59.714
and lots of other little things like that.

00:03:02.071 --> 00:03:03.154
- Thank you all.

00:03:04.121 --> 00:03:07.861
Let's start with Erin and
talking about the big news

00:03:07.861 --> 00:03:10.321
of this week, you just reported

00:03:10.321 --> 00:03:12.081
on the new Omicron variant

00:03:12.081 --> 00:03:14.151
in our coronavirus update newsletter

00:03:14.151 --> 00:03:16.331
and also for our website.

00:03:16.331 --> 00:03:18.751
And what has it been like
covering the emergence

00:03:18.751 --> 00:03:22.891
of these new variant, especially
when, at the beginning,

00:03:22.891 --> 00:03:25.324
there's almost no information available.

00:03:27.891 --> 00:03:28.801
- I mean, in short,

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:31.241
it has been much like the
rest of this pandemic,

00:03:31.241 --> 00:03:33.291
which is to say it is a rollercoaster

00:03:34.393 --> 00:03:35.759
specifically with Omicron,

00:03:35.759 --> 00:03:39.084
I think many of the unknowns
are very much still unknown.

00:03:39.084 --> 00:03:42.738
I think many of the unknowns
are very much still unknown.

00:03:42.738 --> 00:03:44.091
For instance, when I was reporting

00:03:44.091 --> 00:03:47.291
on things like the Alpha
variant that at first emerged

00:03:47.291 --> 00:03:49.381
in the United Kingdom
at the end of last year

00:03:49.381 --> 00:03:54.201
or the Beta variant,
there were some indication

00:03:54.201 --> 00:03:57.374
already in the community of
increased transmissibility.

00:03:58.301 --> 00:03:59.981
There is some indication that Omicron

00:03:59.981 --> 00:04:03.561
may be more transmissible,
but a lot of that data

00:04:03.561 --> 00:04:06.771
is very, very early, so
I would say the unknowns

00:04:06.771 --> 00:04:08.411
are even greater in this case.

00:04:08.411 --> 00:04:13.371
So we're navigating this
very hard to navigate time,

00:04:13.371 --> 00:04:15.421
and it's very hard to report on things

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.401
that we don't have answers for
because people want answers.

00:04:18.401 --> 00:04:20.711
I want to know all the
answers to these questions,

00:04:20.711 --> 00:04:22.231
our readers want to know,

00:04:22.231 --> 00:04:23.791
the scientists themselves want to know,

00:04:23.791 --> 00:04:26.371
but we just don't have the data yet.

00:04:26.371 --> 00:04:28.531
So trying to take a step back

00:04:28.531 --> 00:04:31.681
thinking about why we
don't have answers yet,

00:04:31.681 --> 00:04:35.171
what will it take to get
answers, has been an interesting

00:04:35.171 --> 00:04:38.554
experience, and difficult, I would say.

00:04:39.831 --> 00:04:42.791
- Really difficult that
uncertainty and frustration

00:04:42.791 --> 00:04:47.701
and just not knowing feels
a lot like it did way back

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:52.211
in January 2020 at the very
beginning of the pandemic.

00:04:52.211 --> 00:04:54.791
There was just so much we didn't know.

00:04:54.791 --> 00:04:56.981
John, you wrote our very first story

00:04:56.981 --> 00:04:58.281
on the novel, "Coronavirus,"

00:04:58.281 --> 00:04:59.891
in early January,

00:04:59.891 --> 00:05:01.841
what were you thinking about that then?

00:05:03.221 --> 00:05:05.171
- Yeah, that week,

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:08.191
so I think I published
the story on January 10th

00:05:08.191 --> 00:05:11.731
and that week, my head was
not in the world of virology,

00:05:11.731 --> 00:05:16.731
I was working on this story
about ocean acidification,

00:05:17.091 --> 00:05:20.591
whether it was affecting fish behavior

00:05:20.591 --> 00:05:24.441
and we get this WHO report
that there's this outbreak

00:05:24.441 --> 00:05:27.481
of a mysterious pneumonia
like illness in Wuhan.

00:05:27.481 --> 00:05:32.251
And I was assigned it not out
of any particular expertise

00:05:32.251 --> 00:05:35.014
in virology or epidemics.

00:05:36.451 --> 00:05:41.451
And I remember just trying
to call up a few sources

00:05:41.461 --> 00:05:45.391
and try to get a handle on
what this was and what it meant

00:05:45.391 --> 00:05:49.051
and going back reading the
story now is kind of surreal.

00:05:49.051 --> 00:05:50.851
I have it up here and I just want to read

00:05:50.851 --> 00:05:54.321
the first two sentences, 'cause
I think it's kind of crazy

00:05:54.321 --> 00:05:56.029
the first two sentences, 'cause
I think it's kind of crazy

00:05:56.029 --> 00:05:58.521
to think how little we knew.

00:05:58.521 --> 00:06:00.291
But on January 10th in 2020,

00:06:00.291 --> 00:06:02.651
I wrote, "a mysterious
outbreak of pneumonia

00:06:02.651 --> 00:06:05.191
in Central China has
preliminarily been pegged

00:06:05.191 --> 00:06:07.941
to a new coronavirus, but
the world health organization

00:06:07.941 --> 00:06:09.751
says there's no need to panic.

00:06:09.751 --> 00:06:12.801
Chinese officials have
reported little evidence so far

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:14.381
of human to human transmission,

00:06:14.381 --> 00:06:18.021
but WHO says, making an
epidemic less likely."

00:06:18.021 --> 00:06:22.111
Which in hindsight seems kind of crazy

00:06:23.321 --> 00:06:26.521
but that was our take
back in early January,

00:06:26.521 --> 00:06:30.091
but sort of sum up, I
wasn't really thinking

00:06:30.091 --> 00:06:32.691
that it would explode into what has

00:06:32.691 --> 00:06:35.534
when I first started
reporting on it back then.

00:06:36.811 --> 00:06:41.691
- What's the one piece of
evidence or thing you heard

00:06:41.691 --> 00:06:45.874
that made you realize, oh
gosh, could be really bad.

00:06:46.841 --> 00:06:51.841
- Yeah, I think as sort
of the weeks progressed

00:06:51.881 --> 00:06:53.781
and the sources I was talking to

00:06:54.761 --> 00:06:57.181
went from being sort of very careful

00:06:57.181 --> 00:06:58.721
not to overstate the risk,

00:06:58.721 --> 00:07:02.674
to being a little more
sort of audibly worried,

00:07:03.681 --> 00:07:07.291
my general sense of
worry, sort of crept up

00:07:08.221 --> 00:07:11.511
and then there was actually
a study that Tina flagged

00:07:11.511 --> 00:07:15.231
about a German business
meeting, where it became clear

00:07:15.231 --> 00:07:19.672
that people could spread
the virus before symptoms.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:24.151
So Tina was the person who
made me think, oh gosh,

00:07:24.151 --> 00:07:28.381
this is something we'll
probably be having to deal with

00:07:28.381 --> 00:07:29.891
for quite a while.

00:07:29.891 --> 00:07:33.981
- Righty, yeah, that study out of Germany

00:07:33.981 --> 00:07:38.441
was at this German auto-parts company

00:07:38.441 --> 00:07:41.041
where they really jumped on things

00:07:41.041 --> 00:07:46.041
and that outbreak at that
company told us so many things

00:07:46.141 --> 00:07:48.051
about the virus.

00:07:48.051 --> 00:07:51.681
That was like the thing
for me that really made it

00:07:52.531 --> 00:07:56.589
seem like a big deal
and that this thing was

00:07:56.589 --> 00:07:58.372
going to go crazy

00:08:00.048 --> 00:08:05.048
because they did find
evidence of transmission

00:08:05.056 --> 00:08:10.056
before people had symptoms,
and in a follow-up

00:08:10.371 --> 00:08:14.541
to that initial paper,
they like actually tracked

00:08:14.541 --> 00:08:18.511
the people and they isolated
infectious virus from them

00:08:18.511 --> 00:08:22.861
and they found that even people
who never developed symptoms

00:08:22.861 --> 00:08:24.911
could transmit the virus

00:08:24.911 --> 00:08:29.131
or at least they were
making infectious viruses.

00:08:29.131 --> 00:08:33.454
And then a little later
at the beginning of April,

00:08:34.999 --> 00:08:39.999
the National Academies of
Sciences released a report

00:08:41.241 --> 00:08:46.241
saying that the coronavirus is airborne,

00:08:46.931 --> 00:08:50.231
that it could be transmitted
in aerosol droplets

00:08:50.231 --> 00:08:54.531
that you're out just
sitting there breathing

00:08:54.531 --> 00:08:58.811
or quietly talking and
then you make many more

00:08:58.811 --> 00:09:02.851
after that, so those two
things together were like,

00:09:02.851 --> 00:09:05.404
oh wow, this could be bad.

00:09:06.281 --> 00:09:07.851
- Yeah, oh, wow, indeed.

00:09:07.851 --> 00:09:10.971
And Laura, you were actually
at scientific meetings

00:09:10.971 --> 00:09:13.961
with thousands of other scientists,

00:09:13.961 --> 00:09:15.034
what was that like?

00:09:15.891 --> 00:09:18.221
- Yeah, I mean, looking back,

00:09:18.221 --> 00:09:22.381
I would never have guessed
what would have happened next,

00:09:22.381 --> 00:09:25.761
but it was very much a moment
that I'll never forget.

00:09:25.761 --> 00:09:29.981
I was in a session on
the coronavirus, 8:00 am,

00:09:29.981 --> 00:09:33.991
on Valentine's Day with
my editor Macon Morehouse

00:09:33.991 --> 00:09:36.921
and we were listening to
Trevor Bedford at the Hutch

00:09:36.921 --> 00:09:40.201
and scientists from Data Foundation,

00:09:40.201 --> 00:09:43.891
talk about how much we
don't know about the virus

00:09:43.891 --> 00:09:47.801
and how our estimates at
the time were likely to be

00:09:47.801 --> 00:09:51.070
vast under estimates of how many people

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:54.174
had already been infected
even back in February.

00:09:55.021 --> 00:09:58.081
And so, yeah, just that
feeling of sitting in a room

00:09:58.081 --> 00:10:01.241
with a lot of other people
inside in a big conference

00:10:02.271 --> 00:10:07.098
and there was a sense of kind
of anxiety and fear, really.

00:10:08.421 --> 00:10:10.891
It was the first time
that it really hit home

00:10:10.891 --> 00:10:13.924
with what we're facing,
what we're looking at.

00:10:14.911 --> 00:10:17.944
So yeah, that's a moment I
won't forget from this pandemic.

00:10:19.421 --> 00:10:21.251
- Yeah, it's not often when we're sitting

00:10:21.251 --> 00:10:24.291
covering a scientific meeting
where the dominant emotion

00:10:24.291 --> 00:10:26.074
is fear, that's for sure.

00:10:27.151 --> 00:10:28.934
We have an audience question.

00:10:29.971 --> 00:10:33.631
How do you handle stories
where the information

00:10:33.631 --> 00:10:37.451
you're learning today means
that a story that was written

00:10:37.451 --> 00:10:40.061
only a few months ago is now inaccurate?

00:10:40.061 --> 00:10:42.061
I think that ones that we saw a lot of

00:10:42.061 --> 00:10:44.691
because, especially in the early months,

00:10:44.691 --> 00:10:47.401
there was so much science
coming out so fast

00:10:47.401 --> 00:10:50.111
and things were changing so fast.

00:10:50.111 --> 00:10:52.321
So who wants to talk about
how we as journalists

00:10:52.321 --> 00:10:53.471
answer that?

00:10:53.471 --> 00:10:58.471
- Well, I think we try to
acknowledge what the state

00:10:59.401 --> 00:11:04.401
of the knowledge was before
and what the scientists

00:11:04.421 --> 00:11:08.531
have now done in order to figure out

00:11:08.531 --> 00:11:10.231
what is really the case

00:11:10.231 --> 00:11:13.744
or at least what is our
best understanding now.

00:11:15.582 --> 00:11:20.582
And communicating that
uncertainty is really important

00:11:23.201 --> 00:11:25.891
and it's a very difficult job.

00:11:25.891 --> 00:11:30.131
I think we need to let people in

00:11:30.131 --> 00:11:34.771
on what exactly the scientific process is

00:11:34.771 --> 00:11:39.071
that it builds on itself,
it corrects itself

00:11:39.071 --> 00:11:43.711
and just because the state
of the knowledge changes

00:11:43.711 --> 00:11:47.771
doesn't mean that, that
means we have to throw out

00:11:47.771 --> 00:11:49.954
everything and can't trust anything,

00:11:51.231 --> 00:11:55.311
over time you get a more
concrete understanding

00:11:55.311 --> 00:12:00.311
of the thing and once you
build up a certain level

00:12:00.331 --> 00:12:04.841
of evidence, you can be pretty
confident in the answer,

00:12:04.841 --> 00:12:08.331
but, you know, sometimes mistakes are made

00:12:08.331 --> 00:12:13.331
and I remember at the very
beginning before we knew

00:12:14.461 --> 00:12:17.324
that this was an airborne virus,

00:12:18.701 --> 00:12:23.061
people thought that it was
transmitted mainly by droplets,

00:12:23.061 --> 00:12:25.851
that you would spew out
and then touch a surface

00:12:25.851 --> 00:12:30.411
and touch your face and
then get infected that way.

00:12:30.411 --> 00:12:33.761
And they were saying don't wear masks

00:12:33.761 --> 00:12:37.953
and so one of my husband's coworkers,

00:12:37.953 --> 00:12:40.471
and so one of my husband's coworkers,

00:12:40.471 --> 00:12:45.271
his wife called me and
was like, I bought masks,

00:12:45.271 --> 00:12:48.081
now people are saying
we shouldn't wear masks,

00:12:48.081 --> 00:12:51.861
but they're saying we need
them for the hospital workers.

00:12:51.861 --> 00:12:53.661
But if they protect the hospital workers,

00:12:53.661 --> 00:12:55.691
why won't they protect me?

00:12:55.691 --> 00:12:59.711
And I thought, you know what,
that's a really good question

00:12:59.711 --> 00:13:03.264
and I don't know what to tell you.

00:13:04.821 --> 00:13:09.712
And I still regret from the
very beginning saying that,

00:13:09.712 --> 00:13:12.170
And I still regret from the
very beginning saying that

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:15.821
ordinary people shouldn't wear
masks because it's very clear

00:13:15.821 --> 00:13:19.781
that masks work in our
one layer of protection

00:13:19.781 --> 00:13:24.481
against the virus, and you
just have to own any mistakes

00:13:24.481 --> 00:13:26.297
against the virus, and you
just have to own any mistakes

00:13:26.297 --> 00:13:31.171
and like I said, acknowledge
what the state of the knowledge

00:13:31.171 --> 00:13:34.564
was before and try to move on.

00:13:36.321 --> 00:13:37.754
- Thanks Tina, yeah.

00:13:40.211 --> 00:13:43.921
Again, this has been
such a difficult subject

00:13:43.921 --> 00:13:47.101
for us to cover in so many ways.

00:13:47.101 --> 00:13:49.761
Actually, here's another audience question

00:13:49.761 --> 00:13:51.981
that is a good one for us.

00:13:51.981 --> 00:13:55.941
This listener says he's a print journalist

00:13:55.941 --> 00:13:59.241
and he's very impressed with
the academic credentials

00:13:59.241 --> 00:14:01.661
of the journalist who
write for Science News

00:14:01.661 --> 00:14:06.661
and many of you have a PhD
and other advanced degrees

00:14:08.381 --> 00:14:13.381
and how does that
academic knowledge change

00:14:13.403 --> 00:14:17.203
change how you approach your assignments?

00:14:20.434 --> 00:14:24.621
- I mean, I think for me
it doesn't always affect

00:14:24.621 --> 00:14:25.821
how I approach assignments,

00:14:25.821 --> 00:14:28.351
I do sometimes have to
kind of remove myself

00:14:28.351 --> 00:14:31.751
from what I know just on a regular basis

00:14:32.981 --> 00:14:35.771
to think about what our
readers need to know,

00:14:35.771 --> 00:14:37.981
because my background is in virology

00:14:37.981 --> 00:14:40.161
and my background is in immunology

00:14:40.161 --> 00:14:42.711
and I write a lot about
both of those topics

00:14:42.711 --> 00:14:47.191
and so sometimes I have to
remember that something that

00:14:47.191 --> 00:14:48.281
that is buried in my brain,

00:14:48.281 --> 00:14:52.111
that I know as something
that I think lots of people

00:14:52.111 --> 00:14:55.181
will know, like T-cells are important,

00:14:55.181 --> 00:14:57.404
not everybody knows what a T-cell is.

00:14:58.581 --> 00:15:02.171
These are cells that are in
our body that are on the hunt

00:15:02.171 --> 00:15:04.961
for pathogens that are going to protect us

00:15:04.961 --> 00:15:06.961
and they're really
important for protecting us

00:15:06.961 --> 00:15:11.091
from disease. So taking a step back

00:15:11.091 --> 00:15:15.831
and thinking about what
information is 100% relevant

00:15:15.831 --> 00:15:19.851
to what story I'm writing
and what do our readers,

00:15:19.851 --> 00:15:22.091
what questions are our
readers going to have,

00:15:22.091 --> 00:15:23.091
is really important.

00:15:26.301 --> 00:15:28.041
- Thank you, Erin, that's really helpful,

00:15:28.041 --> 00:15:30.621
especially for people like
me who do not have PhDs

00:15:30.621 --> 00:15:31.454
in virology.

00:15:31.454 --> 00:15:35.411
So I want to ask Aimee a question,

00:15:35.411 --> 00:15:38.811
especially since she
covered a lot of how COVID

00:15:38.811 --> 00:15:43.121
has impacted children,
including how they get sick

00:15:43.121 --> 00:15:44.251
with COVID, what might happen,

00:15:44.251 --> 00:15:48.411
and also how it's affected
schools and learning

00:15:49.521 --> 00:15:53.161
and how is reporting on that

00:15:53.161 --> 00:15:55.911
shaped your perception of
how this is affecting people?

00:15:58.031 --> 00:16:02.221
- Yeah, so, going back
to what Tina was saying,

00:16:02.221 --> 00:16:06.281
our understanding of this
virus continues to change

00:16:06.281 --> 00:16:07.741
over the course of the pandemic.

00:16:07.741 --> 00:16:11.421
And as a reporter, we're
taking in all that information

00:16:11.421 --> 00:16:14.834
and we're continually trying
to make a bigger picture,

00:16:16.501 --> 00:16:19.821
but often the public just gets snapshots

00:16:19.821 --> 00:16:24.792
and I think it's been, it
can be difficult to dislodge

00:16:24.792 --> 00:16:27.783
and I think it's been, it
can be difficult to dislodge

00:16:27.783 --> 00:16:32.781
early impressions that people get,

00:16:32.781 --> 00:16:34.291
before more data has come in

00:16:34.291 --> 00:16:38.791
and before we have gotten
to get that better picture

00:16:38.791 --> 00:16:40.601
and this has been really
true with kids in COVID.

00:16:40.601 --> 00:16:44.621
So, early on, there were
fewer cases for kids

00:16:44.621 --> 00:16:46.561
compared with adults and it wasn't clear

00:16:46.561 --> 00:16:49.283
if kids can really spread COVID

00:16:49.283 --> 00:16:50.351
and that's in contrast to,

00:16:50.351 --> 00:16:54.179
we know kids are really
good spreaders of influenza,

00:16:55.875 --> 00:16:59.011
but that was a time when
kids weren't in school

00:16:59.011 --> 00:17:00.651
and that was a time when
kids weren't in the community

00:17:00.651 --> 00:17:01.881
like they normally are.

00:17:01.881 --> 00:17:03.761
So there's a lot of nuance
there and there's a lot more

00:17:03.761 --> 00:17:04.594
we had to learn.

00:17:04.594 --> 00:17:08.191
But I think early on
this idea that got stuck

00:17:08.191 --> 00:17:09.281
in people's heads that, oh,

00:17:09.281 --> 00:17:12.191
kids aren't affected by this
and that's a comforting thing,

00:17:12.191 --> 00:17:15.721
it's understandable why
people heard that and like,

00:17:15.721 --> 00:17:17.531
okay, good, we're set with that.

00:17:17.531 --> 00:17:21.169
But, we found that wasn't the case.

00:17:21.169 --> 00:17:23.811
As kids got back to
school, as kids got back

00:17:23.811 --> 00:17:25.321
to their activities, to their sports,

00:17:25.321 --> 00:17:27.011
as they were out and about,

00:17:27.011 --> 00:17:29.611
especially as the Delta Variant hit,

00:17:31.441 --> 00:17:33.241
cases in kids went up and with that

00:17:33.241 --> 00:17:35.791
came more hospitalizations and
with that came more deaths.

00:17:35.791 --> 00:17:40.451
And we have learned about
really severe immune response

00:17:40.451 --> 00:17:43.871
that kids can have at
least after infection.

00:17:43.871 --> 00:17:47.088
We've learned, kids can have long COVID.

00:17:47.088 --> 00:17:51.321
So I think it's really
challenging as a reporter,

00:17:51.321 --> 00:17:52.411
all the information coming in

00:17:52.411 --> 00:17:53.881
and you're trying to
create this bigger picture

00:17:53.881 --> 00:17:56.671
that captures a nuance
and tries to help people

00:17:56.671 --> 00:17:59.791
understand, this is what we
understood in the beginning,

00:17:59.791 --> 00:18:02.511
but this is where things are now.

00:18:02.511 --> 00:18:05.391
And that's for many
things about the pandemic,

00:18:05.391 --> 00:18:09.014
that has been a challenge to get out.

00:18:11.811 --> 00:18:13.281
- Thank you Aimee.

00:18:13.281 --> 00:18:17.021
We have a listener who wants to know about

00:18:17.021 --> 00:18:21.651
what process we use to verify
stories before we print them

00:18:21.651 --> 00:18:25.141
and also, what we'll use as resources.

00:18:25.141 --> 00:18:29.661
And that's actually something
we've talked a lot about

00:18:29.661 --> 00:18:33.714
with this because we ended
up using a lot of sources

00:18:33.714 --> 00:18:35.641
that we typically don't rely on,

00:18:35.641 --> 00:18:37.981
we're pretty much obsessed
with accuracy here

00:18:37.981 --> 00:18:41.071
at Science News, and we fact check,

00:18:41.071 --> 00:18:42.961
we have professional fact checkers,

00:18:42.961 --> 00:18:45.491
we do a lot of verification

00:18:45.491 --> 00:18:47.301
and a very careful about our sources,

00:18:47.301 --> 00:18:51.521
but this up ended our
processes in a lot of ways.

00:18:51.521 --> 00:18:52.711
Tina, you had some thoughts on that,

00:18:52.711 --> 00:18:54.431
do you want to weigh in?

00:18:54.431 --> 00:18:57.100
- Yeah, definitely.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:01.621
I've been reporting a
lot on vaccines and drugs

00:19:01.621 --> 00:19:04.391
and a lot of the first
information about those

00:19:04.391 --> 00:19:07.311
comes from press releases
from the companies

00:19:07.311 --> 00:19:08.991
that are making them.

00:19:08.991 --> 00:19:12.011
And so you have very little data to go on,

00:19:12.011 --> 00:19:14.891
you have only the numbers
that they give you

00:19:14.891 --> 00:19:18.091
and they don't tell you much as a whole,

00:19:18.091 --> 00:19:21.891
sometimes they don't even tell
you exactly how many people

00:19:21.891 --> 00:19:24.901
were in the trial, for instance.

00:19:24.901 --> 00:19:28.891
So you have to report that,

00:19:28.891 --> 00:19:33.869
you put a link to the
source and then you talk

00:19:33.869 --> 00:19:37.131
to other experts and try to see,

00:19:37.131 --> 00:19:39.521
does this sound reasonable?

00:19:39.521 --> 00:19:42.283
What are the caveats here?

00:19:42.283 --> 00:19:46.791
And that's also been true for pre-prints.

00:19:46.791 --> 00:19:50.016
So, prior to this, we rarely
would report on pre-prints,

00:19:50.016 --> 00:19:53.099
So, prior to this, we rarely
would report on pre-prints,

00:19:53.099 --> 00:19:57.981
which are papers that are
posted to these internet servers

00:19:57.981 --> 00:20:00.406
which are papers that are
posted to these internet servers

00:20:00.406 --> 00:20:05.361
for anybody to look at and comment on

00:20:05.361 --> 00:20:09.701
that have not been through
the peer review process

00:20:09.701 --> 00:20:14.181
that is normal before papers
appear in scientific journals,

00:20:14.181 --> 00:20:18.611
where other experts in the
field have come through the data

00:20:18.611 --> 00:20:23.611
and seen whether this
is a reasonable thing.

00:20:24.341 --> 00:20:28.871
Peer review has lots of
papers that get retracted

00:20:28.871 --> 00:20:31.171
even after they've gone
through peer review,

00:20:31.171 --> 00:20:36.154
but at least it's one layer of
vetting of a scientific study

00:20:36.154 --> 00:20:40.054
but at least it's one layer of
vetting of a scientific study

00:20:40.054 --> 00:20:43.131
that you don't have when it's a pre-print.

00:20:43.131 --> 00:20:48.001
So you have to be really
diligent about going

00:20:48.001 --> 00:20:52.309
and getting outside
comment and getting people

00:20:52.309 --> 00:20:56.301
who really do know what's
going on in that field

00:20:56.301 --> 00:21:00.831
to take a look at it
and try to help you out,

00:21:00.831 --> 00:21:04.961
tell you where there are
shortcomings and things like that.

00:21:04.961 --> 00:21:09.961
Yeah, we do a lot of fact checking,

00:21:10.551 --> 00:21:15.341
trying to find independent
sources to verify these things,

00:21:15.341 --> 00:21:16.701
it's really not easy,

00:21:16.701 --> 00:21:20.661
especially when there's so
much information coming out

00:21:20.661 --> 00:21:25.106
and so much of it being contradictory.

00:21:25.106 --> 00:21:29.251
You don't really know
exactly what is right

00:21:29.251 --> 00:21:32.781
at the beginning, and like I said earlier,

00:21:32.781 --> 00:21:36.901
sometimes it's maybe better
to wait a little while

00:21:36.901 --> 00:21:41.261
to try and figure out what's really true

00:21:41.261 --> 00:21:44.271
and what's not by the
weight of the evidence

00:21:44.271 --> 00:21:46.631
that's coming in.

00:21:46.631 --> 00:21:48.531
- Yeah, I just wanted to
add something to that,

00:21:48.531 --> 00:21:51.131
Tina mentioned that we're relying a lot

00:21:51.131 --> 00:21:52.561
on pre-prints, which we are,

00:21:52.561 --> 00:21:55.201
but we also have to
remember that sometimes

00:21:55.201 --> 00:21:56.421
even peer reviewed papers,

00:21:56.421 --> 00:21:59.761
we really need to get a lot of
perspective on those as well,

00:21:59.761 --> 00:22:01.701
because peer review is not perfect

00:22:01.701 --> 00:22:04.851
and so getting a lot of,
talking to a lot of scientists

00:22:04.851 --> 00:22:07.041
or talking to a lot of
experts in that field

00:22:07.041 --> 00:22:08.901
to get a sense of even
peer reviewed papers

00:22:08.901 --> 00:22:09.811
is really important.

00:22:09.811 --> 00:22:13.391
I remember the first
coronavirus story that I wrote

00:22:13.391 --> 00:22:16.801
was a few weeks after John wrote his

00:22:16.801 --> 00:22:19.071
and it was a peer reviewed paper

00:22:19.071 --> 00:22:21.181
that said that the
origin of the coronavirus

00:22:21.181 --> 00:22:22.171
was in snakes.

00:22:22.171 --> 00:22:25.721
And all the virologists
that I knew were like,

00:22:25.721 --> 00:22:28.961
this is ridiculous, no,
and I knew as a virologist,

00:22:28.961 --> 00:22:30.941
that it was probably also not true.

00:22:30.941 --> 00:22:34.781
So I talked to a few experts
and who told me the analysis

00:22:34.781 --> 00:22:37.031
that was in this paper is not appropriate

00:22:37.031 --> 00:22:39.621
for the type of conclusions
that they're trying to come to,

00:22:39.621 --> 00:22:43.291
so, I'm taking this with a very,

00:22:43.291 --> 00:22:45.681
with a grain of salt, this
is probably not the case.

00:22:45.681 --> 00:22:49.491
And it ultimately, it
definitely was not the case.

00:22:49.491 --> 00:22:51.681
It was an interesting
experience and I mean,

00:22:51.681 --> 00:22:53.881
that being my first story was,

00:22:53.881 --> 00:22:58.881
taking down a peer reviewed
paper was interesting.

00:22:58.884 --> 00:23:00.764
- Good story.
- Yeah, if can add to,

00:23:02.341 --> 00:23:07.308
I think Science News has
provided a good resource

00:23:07.308 --> 00:23:11.871
in this media environment
where often headlines

00:23:11.871 --> 00:23:14.531
will sort of grab something really catchy

00:23:14.531 --> 00:23:16.141
from an individual study,

00:23:16.141 --> 00:23:19.021
but when you delve into
the details of that study,

00:23:19.021 --> 00:23:20.681
it doesn't really support the headline,

00:23:20.681 --> 00:23:22.481
that's getting sort of blasted

00:23:22.481 --> 00:23:25.791
across a lot of large outlets.

00:23:25.791 --> 00:23:29.631
One example that I remember was in,

00:23:29.631 --> 00:23:33.261
it was in August of 2020,
or sometime around then,

00:23:33.261 --> 00:23:38.081
there was a study that
caused headlines to say that

00:23:38.081 --> 00:23:41.361
neck gaiters were worse
than wearing no mask at all

00:23:41.361 --> 00:23:43.801
because they like sheared the droplets

00:23:43.801 --> 00:23:48.531
as they were coming out of your mouth.

00:23:48.531 --> 00:23:52.154
And a lot of people wear
neck gaiters as masks.

00:23:52.154 --> 00:23:55.961
And so I looked into the
details of that paper

00:23:55.961 --> 00:24:00.651
and it was in no way designed
to answer that question.

00:24:00.651 --> 00:24:05.001
And I think that we provided
a good kind of check

00:24:05.001 --> 00:24:08.451
on this story that had gotten,

00:24:08.451 --> 00:24:11.691
or this paper that had
gotten sort of undue coverage

00:24:11.691 --> 00:24:14.941
by just delving into the
details of that paper

00:24:14.941 --> 00:24:17.774
and saying what they can and can't say.

00:24:19.431 --> 00:24:22.141
- Yeah, I particularly appreciated
your work on that, John,

00:24:22.141 --> 00:24:26.571
because it is pain-taking
reporting and it's also tough

00:24:26.571 --> 00:24:29.941
to come back when all these
other news outlets are saying,

00:24:29.941 --> 00:24:31.794
oh yeah, neck gaiters are
bad and say, well, no,

00:24:31.794 --> 00:24:34.979
actually, what the study says is this,

00:24:34.979 --> 00:24:39.291
and make sure that people
get the accurate information.

00:24:39.291 --> 00:24:43.551
We have a number of listeners
who are curious about COVID

00:24:43.551 --> 00:24:48.431
in the brain and I'd love to
hear from Laura and Sujata

00:24:48.431 --> 00:24:51.811
because you have put a great deal of work

00:24:51.811 --> 00:24:54.521
looking at COVID's effects on the brain,

00:24:54.521 --> 00:24:59.521
on mental health, and
also on how people behave.

00:24:59.551 --> 00:25:02.631
Laura, do you want to start out?

00:25:02.631 --> 00:25:04.081
- Yeah, sure.

00:25:04.081 --> 00:25:05.921
It's, been fascinating

00:25:05.921 --> 00:25:09.911
and like all these other
aspects of the pandemic,

00:25:09.911 --> 00:25:12.441
the science has been really unsettled

00:25:12.441 --> 00:25:17.148
and there's still a lot
more questions than answers.

00:25:17.148 --> 00:25:21.231
The kind of best evidence
so far suggests that

00:25:21.231 --> 00:25:25.251
coronavirus doesn't affect,
infect brain cells directly,

00:25:25.251 --> 00:25:28.153
but it's more, kind of
these systemic effects

00:25:28.153 --> 00:25:32.021
of being severely ill.

00:25:32.021 --> 00:25:35.361
But one thing that's
definitely clear is the stress

00:25:35.361 --> 00:25:38.511
and worry and anxiety
that this has all caused,

00:25:38.511 --> 00:25:40.961
has huge effects.

00:25:40.961 --> 00:25:44.291
And it's been interesting
because that's not a new area

00:25:44.291 --> 00:25:45.271
of neuroscience.

00:25:45.271 --> 00:25:47.981
For a long time, people have known that

00:25:47.981 --> 00:25:52.411
severe long unpredictable
stress can really do a number

00:25:52.411 --> 00:25:56.791
on all sorts of things, our
thinking abilities, our sleep,

00:25:56.791 --> 00:25:59.251
our mental health.

00:25:59.251 --> 00:26:03.061
So we've been kind of following
along and trying to put

00:26:03.061 --> 00:26:06.041
what we're all experiencing
into this context

00:26:06.041 --> 00:26:08.441
of this research that we know.

00:26:08.441 --> 00:26:11.641
So it's been interesting thinking about,

00:26:11.641 --> 00:26:14.381
the features of the pandemic, such as,

00:26:14.381 --> 00:26:16.521
we're all isolated at home,

00:26:16.521 --> 00:26:19.031
we're all wondering what's
going to happen next,

00:26:19.031 --> 00:26:22.451
it feels like a
rollercoaster as Erin said,

00:26:22.451 --> 00:26:26.661
and out of burdens of
homeschooling children

00:26:26.661 --> 00:26:30.841
or extra job duties, it's all
kind of this perfect storm

00:26:30.841 --> 00:26:34.041
of if you were to design
a system to create

00:26:34.041 --> 00:26:37.814
kind of damaging stress
responses, this would be it.

00:26:38.671 --> 00:26:41.591
So, I mean, that's the pessimistic answer.

00:26:41.591 --> 00:26:45.481
I think John might have more optimistic,

00:26:45.481 --> 00:26:47.841
what can we do about it?

00:26:47.841 --> 00:26:50.082
How do we go from here?

00:26:51.361 --> 00:26:52.861
- Well, I'll start with pessimism,

00:26:52.861 --> 00:26:55.131
but maybe I can end it optimism.

00:26:55.131 --> 00:26:56.851
So I remember when I was first studying

00:26:56.851 --> 00:26:59.451
sort of the mental health
fallout of the pandemic,

00:26:59.451 --> 00:27:00.821
there had been no book for it

00:27:00.821 --> 00:27:02.601
and on how we would handle pandemics.

00:27:02.601 --> 00:27:06.031
And there was this expectation
that people would behave

00:27:06.031 --> 00:27:07.211
in a certain way,

00:27:07.211 --> 00:27:10.561
basically they would follow
whatever advice is given,

00:27:10.561 --> 00:27:12.951
but the advice was, as Tina said,

00:27:12.951 --> 00:27:14.891
kind of contradictory, like around masks,

00:27:14.891 --> 00:27:17.307
and then also just people
didn't behave in ways

00:27:17.307 --> 00:27:21.864
that people had predicted.

00:27:21.864 --> 00:27:26.842
And so covering behavior has
been a very interesting journey

00:27:26.842 --> 00:27:29.961
through this whole thing.

00:27:29.961 --> 00:27:32.711
I just remember early on, I covered,

00:27:32.711 --> 00:27:34.351
I spoke to a scientist

00:27:34.351 --> 00:27:36.191
who talks about risk perception a lot

00:27:36.191 --> 00:27:39.041
and she just had this excellent point that

00:27:39.041 --> 00:27:43.261
we know that people who feel more angry,

00:27:43.261 --> 00:27:45.681
are going to flout the rules more, I think

00:27:45.681 --> 00:27:48.321
and people who feel more fearful

00:27:48.321 --> 00:27:50.171
are going to sort of hunker down more

00:27:50.171 --> 00:27:52.081
and that turned out to be so prescient,

00:27:52.081 --> 00:27:54.943
I think in the end.

00:27:54.943 --> 00:27:58.101
And I think personally I
was getting a little down,

00:27:58.101 --> 00:28:00.031
like we were getting this,

00:28:00.031 --> 00:28:02.041
overall I would say
we've gotten the science

00:28:02.041 --> 00:28:05.771
of our understanding of this
virus pretty, pretty solid,

00:28:05.771 --> 00:28:08.441
like we kind of know how it
operates and we keep trying

00:28:08.441 --> 00:28:10.251
to keep up with all these variants,

00:28:10.251 --> 00:28:13.258
but we haven't really done a good job

00:28:13.258 --> 00:28:16.421
on the softer sciences,
I guess, on the behavior

00:28:16.421 --> 00:28:18.841
and the decision-making
and the risk analysis,

00:28:18.841 --> 00:28:22.841
like that's been a lot muddier.

00:28:22.841 --> 00:28:25.701
And so at some point I
think I just took a turn

00:28:25.701 --> 00:28:27.311
to psychology and like,
what can psychology say

00:28:27.311 --> 00:28:30.861
about how we can handle all this noise?

00:28:30.861 --> 00:28:34.921
And I sort of, it was a strange route.

00:28:34.921 --> 00:28:37.978
Like, I was like, well, I
remember how it happened.

00:28:37.978 --> 00:28:40.611
And so reading about
Aristotle for some reason

00:28:40.611 --> 00:28:42.041
And he has these sort of two ideas,

00:28:42.041 --> 00:28:45.711
like people pursue a good
life through happiness

00:28:45.711 --> 00:28:49.151
and, these are lay
terms, or through meaning

00:28:49.151 --> 00:28:51.421
and I knew that people
weren't really happy

00:28:51.421 --> 00:28:54.181
and then I'd also read a paper
that said that we get meaning

00:28:54.181 --> 00:28:57.371
sort of through our routines
and those were really disrupted

00:28:57.371 --> 00:28:58.204
and I'm like, oh great,

00:28:58.204 --> 00:29:00.371
so we're not going to have a good life.

00:29:00.371 --> 00:29:03.161
And then I came across a body of work

00:29:03.161 --> 00:29:05.281
that really just spoke to me

00:29:05.281 --> 00:29:08.931
and it was like novel experiences alone,

00:29:08.931 --> 00:29:10.581
not adding up to anything greater,

00:29:10.581 --> 00:29:13.161
just doing new things could maybe help us

00:29:13.161 --> 00:29:15.561
sort of survive this moment.

00:29:15.561 --> 00:29:16.911
And so I started applying these things

00:29:16.911 --> 00:29:19.481
to my own personal
life, I took up pottery,

00:29:19.481 --> 00:29:22.456
which I read about in one of my stories

00:29:22.456 --> 00:29:25.521
and I started walking
regularly with a friend.

00:29:25.521 --> 00:29:26.791
Initially, it felt so stupid,

00:29:26.791 --> 00:29:29.631
we'd go to the same place on the same day

00:29:29.631 --> 00:29:32.511
and we'd go on the same
exact walk and like months

00:29:32.511 --> 00:29:34.381
into this, I'm like, wow,

00:29:34.381 --> 00:29:36.271
this is really an anchor point in my life.

00:29:36.271 --> 00:29:40.681
So yeah, I don't know, for me,

00:29:40.681 --> 00:29:42.991
that was like a more
optimistic turn that like,

00:29:42.991 --> 00:29:46.121
we can do these things
that can make our lives

00:29:46.121 --> 00:29:48.021
a little bit better, they're small,

00:29:48.021 --> 00:29:52.371
they may not be like
sustainable for like a good life

00:29:52.371 --> 00:29:53.204
for the long haul,

00:29:53.204 --> 00:29:55.851
but hopefully this is not
going to last forever.

00:29:55.851 --> 00:30:00.787
So that's kind of where
I came away with that.

00:30:02.241 --> 00:30:03.821
And Laura and Sujata,

00:30:03.821 --> 00:30:05.961
you had a really interesting conversation

00:30:05.961 --> 00:30:09.721
as part of our session
that was recorded on stress

00:30:09.721 --> 00:30:11.791
in the brain and pandemic,

00:30:11.791 --> 00:30:13.621
where you talked to
both the neuroscientist

00:30:13.621 --> 00:30:17.441
and also a psychiatrist who
works with a lot of populations

00:30:17.441 --> 00:30:18.401
in different countries,

00:30:18.401 --> 00:30:20.511
people who have been in very stressful,

00:30:20.511 --> 00:30:22.681
traumatic situations.

00:30:22.681 --> 00:30:26.891
And I loved looking at
that both in terms of brain

00:30:26.891 --> 00:30:31.871
and society and how we're
still a part of our communities

00:30:31.871 --> 00:30:35.971
and that communities can actually help us

00:30:35.971 --> 00:30:37.854
through these horrible times.

00:30:39.551 --> 00:30:43.259
What did you learn from that conversation?

00:30:44.941 --> 00:30:45.774
- Yeah, I mean,

00:30:45.774 --> 00:30:49.721
it gets to this idea of
we're social creatures,

00:30:49.721 --> 00:30:52.198
we derive so much of our
meaning in our health

00:30:52.198 --> 00:30:56.401
and our success and happiness
from those around us

00:30:56.401 --> 00:30:58.881
and being a part of the community.

00:30:58.881 --> 00:31:01.822
So there have been really well
known effects of isolation,

00:31:01.822 --> 00:31:05.441
in human beings and in animal studies,

00:31:05.441 --> 00:31:08.971
we're not meant to be
alone, we need stimulation,

00:31:08.971 --> 00:31:11.642
we need other people around us.

00:31:11.642 --> 00:31:15.961
And so I think that
discussion and that framework

00:31:15.961 --> 00:31:20.961
of us as a part of a larger
system is really helpful

00:31:20.961 --> 00:31:22.701
and interesting, both in terms

00:31:22.701 --> 00:31:26.431
of kind of how things go wrong,
but also how we can use it

00:31:26.431 --> 00:31:30.341
to buoy ourselves, to support each other

00:31:30.341 --> 00:31:33.901
and to heal a little bit.

00:31:33.901 --> 00:31:35.591
- Yeah, just to add to that.

00:31:35.591 --> 00:31:36.511
Oops, sorry.

00:31:36.511 --> 00:31:37.344
- No, go ahead.

00:31:37.344 --> 00:31:41.181
- I was just going to say
that I found, Brandon Kohrt

00:31:41.181 --> 00:31:43.481
was the researcher I spoke with,

00:31:43.481 --> 00:31:46.511
and I had read a popular science article

00:31:46.511 --> 00:31:48.701
where he talked about PTSD

00:31:48.701 --> 00:31:52.411
and his research really showed
that we tend to treat PTSD

00:31:52.411 --> 00:31:54.301
or post-traumatic stress disorder

00:31:54.301 --> 00:31:57.593
as a sort of individual problem

00:31:57.593 --> 00:32:00.411
manageable with sort of
wellness programs or yoga

00:32:00.411 --> 00:32:03.361
or mindfulness, which
not to knock any of that,

00:32:03.361 --> 00:32:05.291
but his research really showed that

00:32:05.291 --> 00:32:06.951
it's a societal problem

00:32:06.951 --> 00:32:10.031
that when people returning from war

00:32:10.031 --> 00:32:12.401
have a societal support group,

00:32:12.401 --> 00:32:15.241
they actually fare a lot
better than if they're left

00:32:15.241 --> 00:32:17.981
on their own other wellness programs.

00:32:17.981 --> 00:32:20.821
And I thought that was really eye opening

00:32:20.821 --> 00:32:24.501
and just really spoke to how
intentional we need to be

00:32:24.501 --> 00:32:28.121
in this moment where it's
easy to be super distant,

00:32:28.121 --> 00:32:31.271
that we need to continue to
cultivate our communities

00:32:31.271 --> 00:32:34.891
or build new communities and
actively seek out community

00:32:34.891 --> 00:32:36.732
as one of the most important things

00:32:36.732 --> 00:32:40.257
for our overall wellbeing.

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:44.511
- It's so true and I also
loved how your researchers

00:32:44.511 --> 00:32:48.181
talked about empathy as
being incredibly powerful,

00:32:48.181 --> 00:32:50.871
not just receiving empathy,
which you said, yeah,

00:32:50.871 --> 00:32:51.941
that'll make them feel better,

00:32:51.941 --> 00:32:56.831
but also having empathy for
other people and making yourself

00:32:56.831 --> 00:32:59.864
feel better too.

00:33:02.971 --> 00:33:06.781
One thing that I think
has been hard for us all,

00:33:06.781 --> 00:33:09.491
everyone around the world, but
also for us as journalists,

00:33:09.491 --> 00:33:13.301
is being stuck at home,
working from our houses

00:33:13.301 --> 00:33:17.901
and apartments, and as
journalists we love to be out

00:33:17.901 --> 00:33:20.971
in the world and reporting
on what's happening.

00:33:20.971 --> 00:33:23.991
And John, you had the opportunity
to do some field recording

00:33:23.991 --> 00:33:26.481
this summer, on getting vaccines out

00:33:26.481 --> 00:33:28.181
to underserved communities.

00:33:28.181 --> 00:33:30.551
What was that experience
like after being at home

00:33:30.551 --> 00:33:32.951
for so long?

00:33:32.951 --> 00:33:35.641
- Yeah, it was really energizing.

00:33:35.641 --> 00:33:40.641
So I had been kind of
thinking about doing a story

00:33:41.249 --> 00:33:45.571
on vaccine access around that time,

00:33:45.571 --> 00:33:49.575
in late spring and early summer.

00:33:49.575 --> 00:33:53.361
The sort of sense that I
got was that in general,

00:33:53.361 --> 00:33:55.451
people kind of thought that like the US

00:33:55.451 --> 00:33:57.461
was a wash in vaccines

00:33:57.461 --> 00:34:01.211
and if you weren't vaccinated,
that was sort of on you.

00:34:01.211 --> 00:34:03.801
When in fact a lot of communities

00:34:03.801 --> 00:34:07.391
faced a number of
different kinds of barriers

00:34:07.391 --> 00:34:09.371
in getting access.

00:34:09.371 --> 00:34:11.871
And I was interested in
exploring that question

00:34:11.871 --> 00:34:16.501
and a statistic from a
Kaiser Family Foundation Poll

00:34:16.501 --> 00:34:19.041
popped out to me from that time,

00:34:19.041 --> 00:34:21.181
which found that Hispanic adults

00:34:21.181 --> 00:34:25.031
were more than twice as likely
or about twice as likely

00:34:25.031 --> 00:34:28.201
to express a strong
desire to get the vaccine,

00:34:28.201 --> 00:34:30.691
but they hadn't yet gotten
it than other groups.

00:34:30.691 --> 00:34:35.171
And so I started doing some
digging to try to figure out

00:34:35.171 --> 00:34:38.441
why that might be the case, and ended up

00:34:38.441 --> 00:34:43.441
talking to a couple of
medical professionals

00:34:43.584 --> 00:34:48.488
who sort of started this entity
called Unidos Contra COVID

00:34:50.081 --> 00:34:54.341
to try to bring vaccines
to people where they were

00:34:54.341 --> 00:34:58.811
in different Hispanic
communities around Philadelphia.

00:34:58.811 --> 00:35:02.201
And I talked to two people
involved on the phone

00:35:02.201 --> 00:35:06.411
and they were really great conversations.

00:35:06.411 --> 00:35:10.161
This small team was
working sort of overtime

00:35:10.161 --> 00:35:14.809
on a volunteer basis for the most part,

00:35:14.809 --> 00:35:17.251
trying to get vaccines
to these communities

00:35:17.251 --> 00:35:19.851
and setting up sort of pop-up events

00:35:19.851 --> 00:35:22.261
at different community events,

00:35:23.141 --> 00:35:24.751
where they could meet people

00:35:24.751 --> 00:35:28.561
who often aren't sort of
going out of their way

00:35:28.561 --> 00:35:31.691
to get medical care, a lot of people in,

00:35:31.691 --> 00:35:35.151
or some people in these
communities were undocumented

00:35:35.151 --> 00:35:38.561
and so they sort of go
from their home to work

00:35:38.561 --> 00:35:40.511
to a trusted place of worship

00:35:40.511 --> 00:35:44.351
and that's sort of the only
places that they really go

00:35:44.351 --> 00:35:48.091
and this group was
trying to bring vaccines

00:35:48.091 --> 00:35:50.241
to where those people were.

00:35:50.241 --> 00:35:51.991
And I was lucky enough to be able to go

00:35:51.991 --> 00:35:56.238
to one of these events and
actually talk to the people

00:35:56.238 --> 00:35:59.421
who I had talked to the
phone on, the doctors

00:35:59.421 --> 00:36:03.381
and nurses involved in running
these events in person,

00:36:03.381 --> 00:36:06.301
and get sort of a better
sense for who they were

00:36:06.301 --> 00:36:09.091
and why they were doing
what they were doing.

00:36:09.091 --> 00:36:13.311
And also talking to people
who were at that event.

00:36:13.311 --> 00:36:16.481
It happened to be for
a community soccer game

00:36:16.481 --> 00:36:20.201
where there was live music and taco stands

00:36:20.201 --> 00:36:23.351
and other food trucks
and just talking to them

00:36:23.351 --> 00:36:25.921
about why they hadn't gotten vaccinated

00:36:25.921 --> 00:36:29.613
and why they were getting vaccinated now.

00:36:31.132 --> 00:36:34.781
It was really energizing to
just hear from people in person.

00:36:34.781 --> 00:36:38.491
And I think it created a better story

00:36:38.491 --> 00:36:40.281
because I was able to get

00:36:40.281 --> 00:36:45.280
sort of a taste of what that
event was actually like.

00:36:45.280 --> 00:36:46.761
sort of a taste of what that
event was actually like.

00:36:46.761 --> 00:36:50.531
- Yeah, that story kind
of inspired me to John,

00:36:50.531 --> 00:36:55.531
because it was like, it's never
too late to get vaccinated

00:36:55.753 --> 00:37:00.741
and one aspect that you
didn't really touch on here

00:37:00.741 --> 00:37:03.781
is that a lot of people
haven't gotten vaccinated

00:37:03.781 --> 00:37:06.191
because of misinformation.

00:37:06.191 --> 00:37:09.621
And that's been a big theme
also throughout the pandemic,

00:37:09.621 --> 00:37:13.431
is just a lot of misinformation
about the virus itself,

00:37:13.431 --> 00:37:18.423
about vaccines, about treatments

00:37:19.461 --> 00:37:23.061
and so, that's kind of another big part

00:37:23.061 --> 00:37:24.851
of what we do as journalists,

00:37:24.851 --> 00:37:28.771
is trying to give people
accurate information

00:37:28.771 --> 00:37:31.641
and counteract that misinformation

00:37:31.641 --> 00:37:35.051
without inadvertently spreading it,

00:37:35.051 --> 00:37:38.391
which is kind of a challenge.

00:37:38.391 --> 00:37:40.551
- Yeah, we actually got
a great question on that

00:37:40.551 --> 00:37:43.141
from a listener who wanted to know,

00:37:43.141 --> 00:37:45.081
how does your awareness
that you're speaking

00:37:45.081 --> 00:37:47.971
to almost two warring publics,

00:37:47.971 --> 00:37:52.121
the COVID vaccine deniers and
the COVID vaccine encouragers,

00:37:52.121 --> 00:37:55.034
affect the content and
tone of your report?

00:37:56.041 --> 00:37:57.451
And we write for the general public,

00:37:57.451 --> 00:38:00.401
how do you write for the public
when you know that people

00:38:00.401 --> 00:38:02.894
have vastly different
points of view about this?

00:38:03.917 --> 00:38:07.011
- Yeah, some of that is
kind of hard to counteract

00:38:07.011 --> 00:38:12.008
because it's a belief system
as opposed to presenting people

00:38:12.008 --> 00:38:15.271
because it's a belief system
as opposed to presenting people

00:38:15.271 --> 00:38:19.781
with facts and then having
them decide rationally

00:38:19.781 --> 00:38:21.421
based on the facts.

00:38:21.421 --> 00:38:26.421
But as journalists,
that's kind of what we do

00:38:26.461 --> 00:38:31.111
and I recently wrote a
story and a newsletter

00:38:31.111 --> 00:38:33.781
that was adapted into a
story for our website,

00:38:33.781 --> 00:38:37.141
about misinformation
surrounding infertility

00:38:37.141 --> 00:38:39.711
from the vaccine.

00:38:39.711 --> 00:38:42.651
I got very frustrated because every time

00:38:42.651 --> 00:38:44.831
infertility would come up,

00:38:44.831 --> 00:38:48.281
other outlets would say things
like, there's no evidence

00:38:48.281 --> 00:38:51.361
that the vaccine causes infertility.

00:38:51.361 --> 00:38:55.571
Well, while that's true,
it's not nearly strong enough

00:38:55.571 --> 00:38:58.231
because not only is there no evidence

00:38:58.231 --> 00:38:59.811
that it causes infertility,

00:38:59.811 --> 00:39:01.221
there is plenty of evidence

00:39:01.221 --> 00:39:04.161
that it does not cause infertility.

00:39:04.161 --> 00:39:09.108
And so I kind of went through
a rundown of various studies

00:39:09.108 --> 00:39:12.751
that show that it does
not cause infertility.

00:39:12.751 --> 00:39:16.174
It does not cause stillbirths,

00:39:16.174 --> 00:39:21.153
it doesn't harm you, but
getting COVID, actually does.

00:39:21.153 --> 00:39:24.371
I don't know if that
changes anybody's minds,

00:39:24.371 --> 00:39:29.371
but it's what I can do
to be part of a solution

00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:31.696
but it's what I can do
to be part of a solution

00:39:31.696 --> 00:39:33.831
rather than part of the problem

00:39:33.831 --> 00:39:36.889
in spreading misinformation.

00:39:38.391 --> 00:39:39.484
- Thank you, Tina.

00:39:40.351 --> 00:39:42.611
Aimee, I'd like to ask you a question.

00:39:42.611 --> 00:39:44.941
You mentioned earlier that you
spent a lot of time this year

00:39:44.941 --> 00:39:47.781
looking at epidemics of the past,

00:39:47.781 --> 00:39:51.844
as part of our Century of
Science coverage for Centennial.

00:39:52.881 --> 00:39:56.341
And you really came to
find that those experiences

00:39:56.341 --> 00:40:00.091
that people had, before really
having lessons for us now,

00:40:00.091 --> 00:40:01.081
what do you think is important

00:40:01.081 --> 00:40:02.581
for people to know about that?

00:40:05.781 --> 00:40:09.781
- Yeah, so, in working on this,

00:40:09.781 --> 00:40:13.501
for all of the advances that
we've made in our understanding

00:40:13.501 --> 00:40:16.571
of pathogens and the immune system

00:40:16.571 --> 00:40:19.201
and in the development of vaccines,

00:40:19.201 --> 00:40:22.511
which is all truly amazing,

00:40:22.511 --> 00:40:26.191
what really struck me as
I looked back at epidemics

00:40:26.191 --> 00:40:27.121
over the last century,

00:40:27.121 --> 00:40:30.641
going back to the 1918 influenza pandemic,

00:40:30.641 --> 00:40:32.141
is what hasn't changed,

00:40:32.141 --> 00:40:36.181
it hasn't changed nearly enough.

00:40:36.181 --> 00:40:38.971
Epidemics continue to
disproportionately impact

00:40:38.971 --> 00:40:41.541
Black people, Latino
people, Indigenous people,

00:40:41.541 --> 00:40:44.051
and others due to racial discrimination

00:40:44.051 --> 00:40:47.201
and long standing health inequities.

00:40:47.201 --> 00:40:51.461
Epidemics can leave many
children parent-less

00:40:51.461 --> 00:40:55.101
and evidence shows that
without societal support,

00:40:55.101 --> 00:40:57.471
these children face devastating health

00:40:57.471 --> 00:41:00.651
and socioeconomic consequences.

00:41:00.651 --> 00:41:05.084
Epidemics leave communities grieving

00:41:06.331 --> 00:41:10.621
and yet society as a
whole is in a real rush

00:41:10.621 --> 00:41:14.014
to put epidemics behind us.

00:41:15.511 --> 00:41:18.651
Yeah, in the end, epidemics,

00:41:18.651 --> 00:41:20.141
it's not just a story about science,

00:41:20.141 --> 00:41:23.961
it's a story about people
and about suffering

00:41:23.961 --> 00:41:26.361
and about loss.

00:41:27.251 --> 00:41:30.361
And I think we need to
do a much better job

00:41:30.361 --> 00:41:31.311
acknowledging that,

00:41:31.311 --> 00:41:33.051
acknowledging the grief that's out there,

00:41:33.051 --> 00:41:38.051
acknowledging the loss that
people have experienced,

00:41:38.551 --> 00:41:41.031
understanding more
about, what does it mean

00:41:41.031 --> 00:41:43.491
to live through an epidemic?

00:41:43.491 --> 00:41:45.761
What does it mean to
emerge from an epidemic?

00:41:45.761 --> 00:41:49.591
What do we need to do to support people

00:41:52.507 --> 00:41:57.121
and take care of the lingering,

00:41:57.121 --> 00:42:01.537
long lasting physical and mental impacts.

00:42:03.331 --> 00:42:06.421
And it's also, really,

00:42:06.421 --> 00:42:09.601
we are still the public
health cornerstones

00:42:09.601 --> 00:42:11.201
that we rely on with masking,

00:42:11.201 --> 00:42:13.141
with social physical distancing,

00:42:13.141 --> 00:42:18.141
with closing down large public venues.

00:42:18.147 --> 00:42:21.281
with closing down large public venues.

00:42:21.281 --> 00:42:23.601
We started doing that
a long, long time ago.

00:42:23.601 --> 00:42:24.911
These things work for us

00:42:24.911 --> 00:42:29.224
and they're really important
parts of our response.

00:42:49.311 --> 00:42:50.991
- Thank you, Aimee.

00:42:50.991 --> 00:42:53.331
We've got a couple of
questions from listeners

00:42:53.331 --> 00:42:55.989
who want to know about,

00:42:57.191 --> 00:42:59.531
what it's like to be
living through a crisis

00:42:59.531 --> 00:43:02.841
at the same time reporting on it.

00:43:02.841 --> 00:43:06.311
And was it a challenge to
step away from the anxiety

00:43:06.311 --> 00:43:10.897
which we're facing personally,
to report on this issue?

00:43:10.897 --> 00:43:14.171
Who'd like to answer that one?

00:43:14.171 --> 00:43:19.171
- I can start, partly, it's
really hard for me to disconnect

00:43:19.183 --> 00:43:23.161
from anything that's going on.

00:43:23.161 --> 00:43:25.077
I think in part, just because,

00:43:25.077 --> 00:43:26.651
even were I not a journalist,

00:43:26.651 --> 00:43:31.421
I would still be thinking
about this extensively.

00:43:34.924 --> 00:43:36.031
Because of my background,

00:43:36.031 --> 00:43:38.581
a lot of the people that I
interact with on a regular basis

00:43:38.581 --> 00:43:40.811
are in the virology world,

00:43:40.811 --> 00:43:45.811
so even when I'm not at
work, thinking about viruses,

00:43:45.851 --> 00:43:48.361
the people that I talk
to in my daily life,

00:43:48.361 --> 00:43:52.291
I'm also talking about
viruses or the immune system,

00:43:52.291 --> 00:43:55.091
or, my mom is a school
nurse, I'm talking to her

00:43:55.091 --> 00:43:58.801
about what's happening in her school.

00:43:58.801 --> 00:44:01.871
So I don't often get a break,

00:44:01.871 --> 00:44:03.881
but then when I try to
force myself to take a break

00:44:03.881 --> 00:44:07.369
or take a step back, I can't.

00:44:09.043 --> 00:44:11.541
And especially in my classes,

00:44:11.541 --> 00:44:14.981
when I was in grad school,
we hear a lot about,

00:44:14.981 --> 00:44:18.045
we would talk a lot about disease X,

00:44:18.045 --> 00:44:21.141
we learned about pandemics
that had happened in the past

00:44:21.141 --> 00:44:24.481
and what's the future
one going to look like?

00:44:24.481 --> 00:44:26.872
And so the realization
that this is a disease X

00:44:26.872 --> 00:44:30.581
And so the realization
that this is a disease X

00:44:30.581 --> 00:44:34.019
there will be other ones in the future is,

00:44:34.019 --> 00:44:36.781
it's a surreal experience, I think,

00:44:36.781 --> 00:44:38.921
to have gone through
the training that I did

00:44:38.921 --> 00:44:43.451
and then be living through
this and then reporting on it,

00:44:43.451 --> 00:44:46.481
sometimes I feel a little bit dissociated

00:44:46.481 --> 00:44:49.311
from everything and I think part of that

00:44:49.311 --> 00:44:50.591
is probably a defense mechanism,

00:44:50.591 --> 00:44:53.081
so I don't go too far down the path

00:44:53.081 --> 00:44:56.361
or I'm trying to protect
my own mental health,

00:44:56.361 --> 00:44:57.998
like kind of like Laura
and Sujata alluded to,

00:44:57.998 --> 00:45:01.911
they know all of us are struggling.

00:45:01.911 --> 00:45:04.591
I don't know how long
it's going to take for me

00:45:04.591 --> 00:45:09.546
to really kind of grieve
once all of this is over.

00:45:11.371 --> 00:45:14.422
It's one day at a time type thing.

00:45:15.671 --> 00:45:20.301
- Yeah, I hear you about that,
Erin, about being always on,

00:45:20.301 --> 00:45:25.241
because when I'm not
reporting on stuff actively

00:45:25.241 --> 00:45:27.031
and reading all these papers,

00:45:27.031 --> 00:45:31.606
and I'm hearing the news and
then I have family members

00:45:31.606 --> 00:45:33.397
and I'm hearing the news and
then I have family members

00:45:33.397 --> 00:45:35.897
and friends asking me for information

00:45:35.897 --> 00:45:39.951
and friends asking me for information

00:45:39.951 --> 00:45:43.681
and then for advice
about what they should do

00:45:43.681 --> 00:45:45.551
in their daily lives.

00:45:45.551 --> 00:45:48.801
I mean, I have friends on
Facebook who message me,

00:45:48.801 --> 00:45:51.471
and one was asking me,

00:45:51.471 --> 00:45:53.851
should I get my kid vaccinated right away?

00:45:53.851 --> 00:45:56.161
Or should I wait for a while?

00:45:56.161 --> 00:46:00.577
And other ones have asked
me various questions,

00:46:00.577 --> 00:46:02.431
And other ones have asked
me various questions,

00:46:02.431 --> 00:46:06.971
they asked me to vet things
that they see on Facebook

00:46:06.971 --> 00:46:09.991
or in social media, which is good,

00:46:09.991 --> 00:46:13.011
I mean, I'm glad that
they're looking to see

00:46:13.011 --> 00:46:15.851
whether or not what
they're hearing is true,

00:46:15.851 --> 00:46:20.776
but I feel a tremendous
sense of responsibility

00:46:20.776 --> 00:46:23.051
but I feel a tremendous
sense of responsibility

00:46:23.051 --> 00:46:28.001
to make sure that people have
good, accurate information.

00:46:28.001 --> 00:46:32.431
And it can be really hard when

00:46:32.431 --> 00:46:36.441
I don't always know what
to do in my own life.

00:46:36.441 --> 00:46:41.441
So, how can I possibly tell
someone else what to do?

00:46:42.214 --> 00:46:45.351
It's very difficult.

00:46:45.351 --> 00:46:49.231
So yeah, it's been a hard row.

00:46:50.501 --> 00:46:51.816
- We all know too much

00:46:51.816 --> 00:46:53.781
(laughs)

00:46:53.781 --> 00:46:55.705
and too little at the same time.

00:46:55.705 --> 00:46:56.961
- Right, right?

00:46:56.961 --> 00:47:00.481
I mean, and it also intellectually,

00:47:00.481 --> 00:47:03.560
the more you learn about it, it's ,

00:47:03.560 --> 00:47:04.660
there's interesting things about it

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:07.451
and at the same time,
you're also part of this,

00:47:07.451 --> 00:47:09.461
you're part of a pandemic
where people are dying,

00:47:09.461 --> 00:47:12.761
people are sick, and you
are trying to maintain

00:47:12.761 --> 00:47:15.794
people are sick, and you
are trying to maintain

00:47:15.794 --> 00:47:19.451
a kind of, I don't know, you never know

00:47:19.451 --> 00:47:20.601
when it's going to hit you,

00:47:20.601 --> 00:47:24.681
how sad this whole thing is, right?

00:47:24.681 --> 00:47:26.071
You're trying to do your
work and you're trying

00:47:26.071 --> 00:47:27.641
to put information together

00:47:27.641 --> 00:47:30.111
and then something can just strike you

00:47:30.111 --> 00:47:34.226
about how devastating this has been.

00:47:36.471 --> 00:47:38.474
- Yeah, I would say for me is something,

00:47:38.474 --> 00:47:40.411
I love it when people ask me questions

00:47:40.411 --> 00:47:41.801
because that energizes me,

00:47:41.801 --> 00:47:43.231
like when I'm talking to other people

00:47:43.231 --> 00:47:46.908
and people are asking me
opinions about vaccines

00:47:46.908 --> 00:47:51.321
or asking me, okay, this
new variant has come,

00:47:51.321 --> 00:47:54.501
like, what do we know about
how well vaccines work?

00:47:54.501 --> 00:47:57.571
I love being able to
say our immune systems

00:47:57.571 --> 00:47:59.081
are really good at what they do,

00:47:59.081 --> 00:48:01.061
we can trust in our immune system.

00:48:01.061 --> 00:48:05.541
And we can believe that
things are going to be okay

00:48:05.541 --> 00:48:07.661
at the end of the day.

00:48:07.661 --> 00:48:10.561
But at the same time, then when I sit down

00:48:10.561 --> 00:48:12.181
and I have to write the story, I just,

00:48:12.181 --> 00:48:14.691
I feel the weight of everything

00:48:14.691 --> 00:48:16.688
that I'm going to put on the page

00:48:16.688 --> 00:48:18.711
and that people are going to read that.

00:48:18.711 --> 00:48:22.569
And sometimes it's a little bit,

00:48:22.569 --> 00:48:25.151
I freeze because like,
I don't know how to put

00:48:25.151 --> 00:48:29.661
what I just told somebody out
loud into words on the page.

00:48:29.661 --> 00:48:33.524
That's a weird experience.

00:48:34.491 --> 00:48:36.141
- Yeah, and as the editor,

00:48:36.141 --> 00:48:38.271
my job is to make it possible

00:48:38.271 --> 00:48:41.321
for these wonderful
journalists to do their jobs

00:48:41.321 --> 00:48:45.821
and when it's a story like this,

00:48:45.821 --> 00:48:49.731
which is such an important
story and the information

00:48:49.731 --> 00:48:53.161
that we're dealing with, depending on it

00:48:53.161 --> 00:48:56.774
actually could save lives,
it could harm people,

00:48:59.321 --> 00:49:00.271
there is that huge,

00:49:03.221 --> 00:49:05.931
I wish I could say to all
of these wonderful people

00:49:05.931 --> 00:49:09.101
it's okay, fine, go take a long vacation

00:49:09.101 --> 00:49:10.191
and then enjoy yourself.

00:49:10.191 --> 00:49:15.191
But I know that they just,
it's, exhausting as it is,

00:49:15.342 --> 00:49:17.191
they don't want to give up on the story,

00:49:17.191 --> 00:49:20.501
which part of what makes them
as good as what they are,

00:49:20.501 --> 00:49:23.117
but it's hard.

00:49:25.501 --> 00:49:29.851
And on the other side
of that, the flip side,

00:49:29.851 --> 00:49:32.911
we've also talked about what gives us hope

00:49:32.911 --> 00:49:37.121
and I noticed that Sujata
talked about her explorations

00:49:37.121 --> 00:49:38.561
in finding meaning in life,

00:49:38.561 --> 00:49:40.691
both through new scientific research

00:49:40.691 --> 00:49:42.481
and also through her pottery,

00:49:42.481 --> 00:49:45.791
which I thought was a wonderful
way to combine science

00:49:45.791 --> 00:49:47.471
and real life.

00:49:50.451 --> 00:49:53.281
What else gives you hope?

00:49:53.281 --> 00:49:57.011
John, what gives you hope
in the midst of all this?

00:49:57.011 --> 00:49:58.781
- Yeah, all right,

00:49:58.781 --> 00:50:03.052
something that has given
me hope in my reporting,

00:50:03.052 --> 00:50:07.051
'cause we sort of talked to

00:50:07.051 --> 00:50:10.831
and often rely on a
lot of the same sources

00:50:10.831 --> 00:50:12.321
and you can kind of get a sense

00:50:12.321 --> 00:50:16.731
for how tired they're
getting, as they're scientists

00:50:16.731 --> 00:50:21.731
trying to keep figuring this out and

00:50:23.511 --> 00:50:24.531
So in my reporting,

00:50:24.531 --> 00:50:28.371
I've talked to a lot of local
public health officials,

00:50:28.371 --> 00:50:31.311
whether that be like a city official,

00:50:31.311 --> 00:50:35.521
who's trying to run public
health programs or school nurses

00:50:35.521 --> 00:50:39.781
trying to set up testing
programs for their school

00:50:39.781 --> 00:50:44.781
and talking with them, I
always feel a bit more hopeful

00:50:44.869 --> 00:50:49.828
knowing that there are these
people who are really working

00:50:51.438 --> 00:50:56.391
against incredible challenges
and often amidst a public

00:50:56.391 --> 00:51:00.761
who either sort of questions
or outright rejects

00:51:00.761 --> 00:51:05.761
what they're trying to
do and sort of the work

00:51:05.771 --> 00:51:10.771
that they're doing and, I
mean, it's really hard on them

00:51:10.813 --> 00:51:14.701
and yet, a lot of the
public health workers

00:51:14.701 --> 00:51:17.711
who I've spoken with over
the course of this pandemic,

00:51:17.711 --> 00:51:22.511
just continue to kind of
do their best to build up

00:51:22.511 --> 00:51:24.271
a testing program for their school,

00:51:24.271 --> 00:51:28.119
or try to find new and creative
ways of reaching people

00:51:28.119 --> 00:51:32.121
who haven't yet been vaccinated.

00:51:32.121 --> 00:51:34.481
And it gives me a little bit of hope

00:51:34.481 --> 00:51:36.361
to know that there are
people out there like that

00:51:36.361 --> 00:51:41.361
who are still plugging away
against incredible odds.

00:51:41.921 --> 00:51:43.401
- Yeah, it gives me hope to know

00:51:43.401 --> 00:51:47.361
that there are these
amazing scientists out there

00:51:47.361 --> 00:51:49.731
who are, they're working on vaccines,

00:51:49.731 --> 00:51:51.521
they're working on treatments,

00:51:51.521 --> 00:51:53.581
they're working to better
understand the virus

00:51:53.581 --> 00:51:57.331
and what it does to our bodies,
and how our immune systems

00:51:57.331 --> 00:52:00.514
react, and also, just to
see, something we touched on

00:52:00.514 --> 00:52:04.281
react, and also, just to
see, something we touched on

00:52:04.281 --> 00:52:07.621
in the session that Erin and I had earlier

00:52:07.621 --> 00:52:10.321
with four COVID scientists,

00:52:10.321 --> 00:52:14.291
about how collaborative
people have gotten,

00:52:14.291 --> 00:52:19.278
there are these huge
collaborations that have hundreds,

00:52:19.278 --> 00:52:23.221
if not thousands of scientists
from all over the world,

00:52:23.221 --> 00:52:27.321
putting their brain power
to solve these problems.

00:52:27.321 --> 00:52:31.071
And it's amazing how
much they have learned

00:52:31.071 --> 00:52:33.111
in such a short time.

00:52:33.111 --> 00:52:35.751
It seems like it's never enough of course,

00:52:35.751 --> 00:52:40.114
but just the fact that they're on the job,

00:52:41.131 --> 00:52:43.824
that's very hopeful for me.

00:52:45.071 --> 00:52:47.351
- Yeah, I want to say I kind
of think of it as great,

00:52:47.351 --> 00:52:49.504
what I'm grateful for,

00:52:49.504 --> 00:52:52.051
I'm grateful for all the doctors
and medical practitioners

00:52:52.051 --> 00:52:55.661
who have been caring for
people throughout this,

00:52:55.661 --> 00:52:58.561
also starting off with
almost no information

00:52:58.561 --> 00:53:02.181
and very little they could
do except provide the care

00:53:02.181 --> 00:53:04.871
that you provide for someone who has,

00:53:04.871 --> 00:53:08.001
is experiencing the symptoms you know.

00:53:08.001 --> 00:53:11.081
And I'm grateful as John was saying,

00:53:11.081 --> 00:53:14.311
for all the public health
officials that have been working

00:53:14.311 --> 00:53:18.371
so hard, and I'm grateful for vaccines,

00:53:18.371 --> 00:53:21.851
that we have something that
can give our immune systems,

00:53:21.851 --> 00:53:24.331
a preview of a pathogen,
something that could protect us

00:53:24.331 --> 00:53:28.251
from the worst effects, is
incredible and it's not something

00:53:28.251 --> 00:53:31.301
that we have for everything,
it's not something we've had

00:53:31.301 --> 00:53:33.509
in the past.

00:53:35.817 --> 00:53:36.824
It's amazing.

00:53:41.351 --> 00:53:43.401
- To expand on that last point Aimee,

00:53:43.401 --> 00:53:45.591
it's just, that's also the
thing that gives me hope,

00:53:45.591 --> 00:53:50.461
is that the scientists developed
the vaccine so quickly,

00:53:50.461 --> 00:53:55.111
thanks to decades of research
that has gone into all of this

00:53:55.111 --> 00:53:58.491
well before the pandemic even began.

00:53:58.491 --> 00:54:00.161
So, all of that work that they pulled on

00:54:00.161 --> 00:54:04.611
to make these vaccines within
a year is just mind boggling.

00:54:04.611 --> 00:54:08.551
And sometimes I sit down and I think about

00:54:08.551 --> 00:54:11.381
where we might be in the pandemic

00:54:11.381 --> 00:54:13.281
if we did not have vaccines

00:54:13.281 --> 00:54:16.831
and it would be a very, very
different place right now.

00:54:16.831 --> 00:54:19.701
I mean, there's obviously a lot of hurdles

00:54:19.701 --> 00:54:22.221
that we're still trying to
overcome, vaccine inequity,

00:54:22.221 --> 00:54:27.211
vaccine hesitancy, just
getting vaccines to people

00:54:27.211 --> 00:54:28.851
who want them, but can't get them.

00:54:28.851 --> 00:54:31.381
I mean, those are big things
that we have to overcome,

00:54:31.381 --> 00:54:33.851
but the fact that we even
have them in the first place

00:54:33.851 --> 00:54:37.034
to be having those
conversations is incredible.

00:54:38.961 --> 00:54:40.681
- Yeah, I was going to add that too,

00:54:40.681 --> 00:54:43.064
it didn't have to work out this well,

00:54:44.301 --> 00:54:48.151
I'm just so incredibly grateful
and shocked that it worked

00:54:48.151 --> 00:54:49.842
I'm just so incredibly grateful
and shocked that it worked

00:54:49.842 --> 00:54:53.721
this well, that they're
so incredibly effective.

00:54:53.721 --> 00:54:57.811
I've had my little daughters
get vaccinated last weekend

00:54:57.811 --> 00:55:02.421
and it was just this
moment of extreme gratitude

00:55:02.421 --> 00:55:04.651
and more broadly, I
think what gives me hope

00:55:04.651 --> 00:55:08.211
is this famous and cheesy Mr. Rogers line

00:55:08.211 --> 00:55:10.981
of "look for the helpers,
look for the helpers here"

00:55:10.981 --> 00:55:13.191
and that includes those scientists,

00:55:13.191 --> 00:55:15.671
that includes all our healthcare workers,

00:55:15.671 --> 00:55:18.051
that includes all of
our cafeteria workers,

00:55:18.051 --> 00:55:21.091
who've been making meals
for kids who need them,

00:55:21.091 --> 00:55:24.241
that includes you guys
covering all of the science

00:55:24.241 --> 00:55:26.931
and giving people the information we need.

00:55:26.931 --> 00:55:29.551
So, yeah, that's where I am.

00:55:29.551 --> 00:55:30.919
- And teachers,

00:55:30.919 --> 00:55:32.188
(laughs)

00:55:32.188 --> 00:55:35.591
(mumbles) having to come back

00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:39.381
in really uncertain environments

00:55:39.381 --> 00:55:43.027
as the return to school happened.

00:55:46.571 --> 00:55:50.187
- Thank you all, those are
such heartfelt reflections

00:55:50.187 --> 00:55:51.878
- Thank you all, those are
such heartfelt reflections

00:55:51.878 --> 00:55:56.861
and I'm looking at one more
comment here from a listener

00:55:58.151 --> 00:56:00.411
that says, since we're
celebrating a hundred years

00:56:00.411 --> 00:56:03.161
of Science News, I would
be interested to see

00:56:03.161 --> 00:56:05.571
a retrospective of how
science and journalists

00:56:05.571 --> 00:56:07.551
a hundred years ago covered the last stage

00:56:07.551 --> 00:56:10.568
stage of the Spanish flu in 1921.

00:56:10.568 --> 00:56:13.151
And I think that's a great question

00:56:13.151 --> 00:56:17.481
and you can start by looking
at Aimee's wonderful story

00:56:17.481 --> 00:56:19.421
on our Century of Science page,

00:56:19.421 --> 00:56:21.418
which is looking at
specific scientific examples

00:56:21.418 --> 00:56:25.701
of the past hundred years
and also includes highlights

00:56:25.701 --> 00:56:30.511
of our coverage, and you can
also dig into our archives

00:56:30.511 --> 00:56:33.201
on our sciencenews.org website,

00:56:33.201 --> 00:56:36.826
which has more than 80,000
articles of original coverage,

00:56:36.826 --> 00:56:39.342
which has more than 80,000
articles of original coverage,

00:56:39.342 --> 00:56:41.591
which you can see exactly
what it looked like

00:56:41.591 --> 00:56:44.031
on the page back then.

00:56:44.031 --> 00:56:48.271
And this spring, we're
going to also do a history

00:56:48.271 --> 00:56:50.791
of our reporting over the years,

00:56:50.791 --> 00:56:54.361
which includes some of the
amazing things in science

00:56:54.361 --> 00:56:58.811
that we've covered, whether
it's the discovery of the DNA

00:56:58.811 --> 00:57:03.451
double helix or insulin or
antibiotics or the Space Race,

00:57:03.451 --> 00:57:07.251
but also some places where we went wrong

00:57:07.251 --> 00:57:08.661
and science went wrong, too,

00:57:08.661 --> 00:57:11.801
and covering things that
turned out to be dangerous

00:57:11.801 --> 00:57:13.361
or wrong or unethical.

00:57:13.361 --> 00:57:18.361
So I encourage you to stick
with us and look forward,

00:57:18.411 --> 00:57:22.341
we've got a lot more coming
as part of our celebration

00:57:22.341 --> 00:57:23.614
of our Centennial.

00:57:24.561 --> 00:57:26.901
And I am hopeful and grateful

00:57:26.901 --> 00:57:30.161
because of our great
journalists and many others

00:57:30.161 --> 00:57:33.284
around the country.

00:57:33.284 --> 00:57:35.941
We just had someone post a comment saying

00:57:35.941 --> 00:57:38.921
how wonderful it was that
people were able to move

00:57:38.921 --> 00:57:42.531
from physical to virtual
socialization and work

00:57:42.531 --> 00:57:45.231
and do concerts, look, school, play date,

00:57:45.231 --> 00:57:48.951
all providing social
interaction in ways online.

00:57:48.951 --> 00:57:52.443
That's true, I think at
the time it didn't seem

00:57:52.443 --> 00:57:54.751
like it would be nearly the compensation

00:57:54.751 --> 00:57:55.791
for not being together,

00:57:55.791 --> 00:58:00.051
but I know that all that virtual
communication last winter

00:58:00.051 --> 00:58:01.241
really helped me get through this

00:58:01.241 --> 00:58:04.051
and I know it did for many others, so.

00:58:05.271 --> 00:58:08.871
I would like to say thank you again

00:58:08.871 --> 00:58:10.951
to our amazing journalists
and the work they do

00:58:10.951 --> 00:58:13.061
and thank you to our great audience

00:58:13.061 --> 00:58:15.071
of Science News readers online

00:58:15.071 --> 00:58:18.119
and in print for supporting us

00:58:18.119 --> 00:58:22.051
and, anybody have a last
comment you want to say

00:58:22.051 --> 00:58:24.696
before we sign off?

00:58:31.831 --> 00:58:36.761
All right, please stick
with us and see what we do

00:58:36.761 --> 00:58:41.153
at our next a hundred
years of science coverage.

00:58:41.153 --> 00:58:43.541
Thank you all.

